        "title": "fix(tool-policy-audit): use truncateUtf16Safe for audit field truncation",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nTool-policy audit fields were truncated with a raw UTF-16 slice. If the 160-code-unit boundary fell inside an emoji or another supplementary character, audit messages and structured metadata could contain a lone surrogate.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAudit-field truncation now uses the shared normalization-core `truncateUtf16Safe` primitive through its direct package subpath. The surrounding control-character escaping, field cap, and `...` marker remain unchanged.\n\nA production-path regression drives an over-limit policy label through `applyToolPolicyPipeline` and verifies both the rendered audit message and metadata at a split surrogate boundary.\n\n## User Impact\n\nTool-policy audit logs no longer contain malformed UTF-16 when long tool names, config labels, or matched rules place a supplementary character at the truncation boundary.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact head: `a8929df7790a49b69246391f7a37e3246189ff47`\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/tool-policy-pipeline.test.ts` — 1 file, 32 tests passed.\n- Targeted oxfmt and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Fresh full-branch autoreview: clean, no accepted/actionable findings (`0.99`).\n\nLocal narrow test fallback was used because fork-contributor code is not eligible for credential-hydrated Testbox execution before landing review.\n\nAI-assisted; maintainer-reviewed.\n",
